  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1152 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nA calm night is being witnessed across the Mid-South with a few\npockets of cirrus trailing behind today's cold front. As clouds\ncontinue to dissipate behind the front and winds go calm along \nnarrow dewpoint depressions, there exists another pretty decent\nsignal for patchy fog development. Best fog chances will exist in\nlow-lying areas of West Tennessee and northeast Mississippi. Any\nfog that does develop will quickly mix out shortly after sunrise.\nThe aforementioned front will also result in a \"cooldown\" Friday\nwith highs spanning the upper 80s to lower 90s. Dry conditions\nand lowered dewpoints will also be present as dry air continues\nto filter in aloft from the Midwest.\n\nSaturday, upper-level subtropical ridging, currently centered\njust south of the four corners region, will begin to broaden\neast. A warming trend will begin with all of the Mid-South seeing\nhighs in the 90s this weekend. With dry air in place from the\nMidwest, our area will see little to no precipitation over the\nnext seven days. Come Monday, ensembles continue to hint at the\naforementioned ridge nearly centering over us at a forecasted\n594dam. Highs will be in the mid to upper 90s beginning Sunday\nand extending through at least mid-week. The good news is that\ndewpoints don't look to surge with forecast values in the upper\n60s to low 70s next week. This will likely keep us away from\noppressive heat. Though some areas, especially in the Delta\nRegion, may necessitate Heat Advisories as early as Monday with\nheat indices potentially reaching 105+F. The latest LREF guidance\nindicates 20-30% chance of hitting indices 105+F in these areas,\nthough this will primarily be driven and depend on dewpoints. A\nrelief from heat does not look likely anytime soon as the latest\n8-14 day outlook indicates a 70-80% chance of the Mid-South being\nabove normal through the first week of September.\n\nAEH",
